WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.385
Django's ORM provides safer patterns for
common database operations by separating

2
00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:11.248
application values from query
construction.

3
00:00:11.328 --> 00:00:17.437
That is a strong starting point, but it
does not remove every database risk.

4
00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:23.821
Raw SQL, dynamic filters, user-controlled
ordering, complex annotations,

5
00:00:23.901 --> 00:00:27.780
and custom query construction deserve
review.

6
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:33.582
Parameterized queries and safe ORM usage
help prevent SQL injection,

7
00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:40.159
while clear authorization filters help
ensure a query only returns records the

8
00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.276
caller is allowed to see.

9
00:00:42.356 --> 00:00:47.496
Database security also includes
migrations, schema changes,

10
00:00:47.576 --> 00:00:53.782
transaction behavior, error handling, and
database account permissions.

11
00:00:53.862 --> 00:00:59.293
A migration can accidentally expose or
transform sensitive data.

12
00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:04.513
A failed transaction can leave a workflow
in a confusing state.

13
00:01:04.593 --> 00:01:11.478
A database user with excessive privileges
can increase the impact of an application

14
00:01:11.558 --> 00:01:12.043
flaw.

15
00:01:12.123 --> 00:01:18.524
The web application's data access should
match the least capability it needs,

16
00:01:18.604 --> 00:01:25.198
and sensitive operations should have
predictable rollback and error behavior.

17
00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:32.164
Mass assignment and over-posting concepts
are important in model-backed forms and

18
00:01:32.244 --> 00:01:33.407
serializers.

19
00:01:33.487 --> 00:01:37.463
If user-submitted fields are bound too
broadly,

20
00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:43.653
a caller may influence fields that should
be controlled only by the server,

21
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:48.776
such as ownership, role, status, pricing,
or approval state.

22
00:01:48.856 --> 00:01:55.547
Defensive code explicitly chooses which
fields can be set from a request and which

23
00:01:55.627 --> 00:02:02.416
fields are derived from authenticated
context, workflow, or server-side policy.

24
00:02:02.496 --> 00:02:06.666
Convenience should not decide the trust
boundary.

25
00:02:06.746 --> 00:02:12.856
File handling and serialization create
additional paths into the system.

26
00:02:12.936 --> 00:02:19.045
Uploads require size limits, safe names,
constrained storage locations,

27
00:02:19.125 --> 00:02:25.041
content handling rules, and authorization
around access and deletion.

28
00:02:25.121 --> 00:02:31.812
Media files and static files serve
different purposes and should not be confused.

29
00:02:31.892 --> 00:02:38.487
JSON, YAML, XML, and CSV processing should
use safe parsers, reasonable limits,

30
00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:40.118
and clear schemas.

31
00:02:40.198 --> 00:02:46.793
Unsafe deserialization at a high level is
the risk of turning untrusted data into

32
00:02:46.873 --> 00:02:51.140
behavior or objects the application did
not intend.
